# Everything in this repository is hash-anchored, so the one thing git must never do is change a
# byte on the way in or out of a checkout. Line-ending translation does exactly that, silently and
# per-platform: with core.autocrlf=true a Windows clone rewrites every text file to CRLF, which
# changes its sha256 and puts a stray \r on the end of every filename inside a checksum manifest.
# The symptom is bewildering rather than obvious -- `sha256sum -c` reports
#
#     sha256sum: 'src/script.cpp'$'\r': No such file or directory
#
# for a file plainly sitting right there. Every EVIDENCE_MANIFEST, every SHA256SUMS and
# MANIFEST.sha256 were affected, so none of the R3/R4 evidence verified on a Windows clone even
# though all of it is correct and every stored blob is LF.
#
# Hence: translate nothing by default. The repo's bytes are the repo's bytes.
* -text

# The exception is executable scripts, normalised the other way -- forced to LF -- because a CRLF
# shell script does not merely hash differently, it fails to run: bash never matches a heredoc
# terminator written as `TXT\r` and reports only "unexpected end of file". Pinning them means a
# script committed from Windows still runs when it lands on a droplet, and pins them identically
# on every platform so the checksums above stay stable either way.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf

# Belt and braces for formats that must never be touched even if the default above is relaxed:
# the evidence images, the archives, the binaries, the signatures, the block data.
*.exe    binary
*.dll    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.gz     binary
*.zip    binary
*.dat    binary
*.asc    binary
*.sig    binary
*.bundle binary
